  ],
  "text": "AI can pass professional exams and still fail to reliably pick up a towel. That is backwards from what everybody expected, and it has a name. Moravec's paradox. Reasoning is cheap. Perception and movement are brutally expensive. Because evolution spent hundreds of millions of years on moving through a physical world. And a few tens of thousands on anything you would call abstract thought. So the hard problems are the ones you do without noticing you are doing them. A towel has no fixed shape. It has folds you cannot see, and it changes as you touch it. There is no clean list of what a towel currently looks like. There is one for a chess board. Which flips the usual prediction about which jobs are actually safest. It is not the ones needing a degree. It is the ones needing hands, in a room nobody tidied. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
